ing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066185/assets/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161856/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "091b8235-56d8-5a08-bc95-08c99772cfd8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161856"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161873/selection/d59490a47d4ce00fe407728b0a511c1e2ca6181e8c591596b31e9c207dae9eec" :answer/id #uuid "a8fc70f0-c0c1-51aa-a411-c59cfb97a1d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161873/selection/b88aa38d3c80350865da3553ed29b03137d7a971c8b59c5ca98013c5664608a1" :answer/id #uuid "c684e0eb-7993-5acb-9c53-331e6b7f10a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161873/selection/d89307d19437505c1698e400d33cece477d6908a50995f0bff19de677acbabd8" :answer/id #uuid "d894cb69-b0c6-5a21-88c8-bbbb12846405" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161873/selection/6c91165959f60f73822920a99b498201f872c904483c69b9186d59647a2c742f" :answer/id #uuid "4cc7022d-903a-524c-a8ea-addf0b82c712" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161873/selection/90207743e1fadd651d3211efdb363c19aea64e8a674515331c35cb7f04186afe" :answer/id #uuid "e5908624-e088-5502-9da6-fc83b968a98b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161873/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b92581a9-0527-5110-b05f-748971ebd883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161873/selection/d89307d19437505c1698e400d33cece477d6908a50995f0bff19de677acbabd8" :answer-field/choices ["q-161873/selection/d59490a47d4ce00fe407728b0a511c1e2ca6181e8c591596b31e9c207dae9eec" "q-161873/selection/b88aa38d3c80350865da3553ed29b03137d7a971c8b59c5ca98013c5664608a1" "q-161873/selection/d89307d19437505c1698e400d33cece477d6908a50995f0bff19de677acbabd8" "q-161873/selection/6c91165959f60f73822920a99b498201f872c904483c69b9186d59647a2c742f" "q-161873/selection/90207743e1fadd651d3211efdb363c19aea64e8a674515331c35cb7f04186afe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161873" :question/id #uuid "9c4b0cb3-e283-5cdd-b9d4-c83ef946d645" :question/math-academy-id "q-161873" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}v\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & -1 \\\\ 2uv & {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}})\\cdot ({u}^{2})-2uv\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1+2uv \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,2)} & =(-1+2uv)\\,{|}_{u=2\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-1+2(2)(2) \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|7|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =7\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161873/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161873"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
